--TEST--
Spec § 1: `Core\Str::replace` and the two pad members each have a bound at which they do nothing, asserted there and at the first input past it
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `replace`'s `{limit}` counts occurrences, and `0` means exactly what it says
// — replace nothing — because a sentinel `0` for "no limit" would have been a
// magic value. So the bound is the occurrence count: 3 `a`s in `banana`, and a
// limit at or past 3 is the whole substitution.
echo "[", Core\Str::replace("banana", "a", "-", {limit: 0}), "]\n";
echo "[", Core\Str::replace("banana", "a", "-", {limit: 3}), "]\n";
echo "[", Core\Str::replace("banana", "a", "-", {limit: 4}), "]\n";

// An empty search is the other input that replaces nothing: not the
// replacement inserted between every character, and not a loop that never
// ends. PHP returns the subject unchanged too.
echo "[", Core\Str::replace("banana", "", "-"), "]\n";

// A pad's bound is the width the subject already has: at it and below it the
// subject comes back untouched, and the first width above it is one piece of
// padding. Neither member ever truncates, which is what makes the bound
// one-sided in effect and two-sided in the argument.
echo "[", Core\Str::padStart("abc", 2, "-"), "][", Core\Str::padStart("abc", 3, "-"), "][", Core\Str::padStart("abc", 4, "-"), "]\n";
echo "[", Core\Str::padEnd("abc", 2, "-"), "][", Core\Str::padEnd("abc", 3, "-"), "][", Core\Str::padEnd("abc", 4, "-"), "]\n";

// Empty padding is refused only where it would have to be *used*: a subject
// already at the width never reaches the padding at all, and the width one
// past it is the first that throws.
echo "[", Core\Str::padStart("abc", 3, ""), "]\n";
try {
    echo Core\Str::padStart("abc", 4, ""), "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $unusable) {
    echo $unusable->message, "\n";
}

// The width is counted in `rule:types/string-is-utf8`'s unit, like every other length in this
// class, so `cafe` + a combining acute is four wide and not five: padding it
// to four is the identity and to five adds exactly one piece. A byte-counted
// pad would have called it five already and added nothing.
echo Core\Str::length("cafe\u{301}") as string, "|",
     Core\Str::length(Core\Str::padStart("cafe\u{301}", 4, "-")) as string, "|",
     Core\Str::length(Core\Str::padStart("cafe\u{301}", 5, "-")) as string, "\n";
--EXPECT--
[banana]
[b-n-n-]
[b-n-n-]
[banana]
[abc][abc][-abc]
[abc][abc][abc-]
[abc]
Core\Str::padStart(): the padding is empty, so it can never reach the requested length
4|4|5
